    story: `Ilyas is named directly in the Quran across two brief but pointed passages, sent, according to the account preserved there, to a specific community that had adopted a particular idol named Ba'l as an object of worship — a deity widely associated in the broader ancient Near Eastern religious landscape with fertility and agricultural abundance, whose worship had, in Ilyas's community, entirely displaced the recognition of the actual Creator responsible for the very abundance the idol was falsely credited with providing.

His confrontation with his people is delivered in the Quran with the same essential structure that runs through every Prophet's opening address across this entire tradition — grounding the call first and foremost in the demand to abandon false worship for the sake of the One who alone deserves it — but framed here with a particularly pointed rhetorical question exposing the specific absurdity of what his people had chosen to worship: "And indeed, Ilyas was from among the messengers. When he said to his people, 'Will you not fear Allah? Do you call upon Ba'l and leave the best of creators — Allah, your Lord and the Lord of your first forefathers?'" (Quran 37:123-126)

The phrase "the best of creators" contains within it the entire force of Ilyas's argument: Ba'l, whatever role his worshippers imagined for him, could create nothing at all, while Allah alone was responsible for the creation not only of the present community but of every single generation of their ancestors who had come before them — a lineage of dependence on the true Creator stretching back through every forefather they themselves could name, contrasted against an idol incapable of creating so much as a single grain of the very crops it was falsely credited with providing.

As with nearly every earlier Prophet recorded in this same sequence, his people's response was rejection rather than acceptance, and the Quran, characteristically, does not linger on an extended description of the specific punishment that ultimately overtook them, focusing instead, as it does with the accounts of several of the shorter prophetic mentions, on the outcome for the believers who followed him and on the honor Allah extended to Ilyas himself in its aftermath: "And they denied him, so indeed, they will be brought [for punishment], except the chosen servants of Allah [among them]." (Quran 37:127-128)

What follows is a passage of blessing extended to Ilyas that closely parallels, in both its wording and its structure, blessings the same surah extends to other Prophets across its verses — a recurring formula the Quran uses specifically for its most honored figures, closing each of their accounts with a declaration that their memory will be preserved and spoken of with peace across every subsequent generation: "And We left for him [favorable mention] among later generations: 'Peace upon Ilyas.' Indeed, We thus reward the doers of good. Indeed, he was among Our believing servants." (Quran 37:129-132)

Later Islamic tradition, drawing on earlier Judeo-Christian material concerning the figure of Elijah with whom Ilyas is generally identified, has preserved additional narrative detail regarding his specific era, his confrontations with a corrupt ruling household, and accounts of miraculous provision during periods of drought — material the classical Quranic commentators, including Ibn Kathir, generally treated with the same careful methodological distinction the tradition applies throughout to material drawn from outside sources: useful and often illuminating as supplementary context, but held to a standard distinctly lower than the direct, authenticated content of the Quran and confirmed hadith themselves, and reported candidly as such rather than presented with equal certainty.

What the Quran itself establishes about Ilyas with full and unambiguous certainty is, in the end, both simple and complete: he was sent as a genuine messenger to call his own specific people away from a worship that credited a lifeless idol with abundance only the true Creator could provide, he was rejected by the overwhelming majority of that same people, and he is honored, in the same breath and the same formula extended to only the most select figures across the Quran's entire prophetic roster, with a peace pronounced upon his name that the text itself declares will be repeated by every generation of believers that comes after him.`,
